= Thermodynamics =

== Overview ==
'''Thermodynamics''' studies energy, heat transfer, work, temperature, entropy and the constraints governing macroscopic physical processes. It uses state variables and relations between systems and their surroundings to analyze changes without necessarily tracking every microscopic particle.

== System, surroundings and state ==
A thermodynamic [[Related to::System boundary|system boundary]] establishes which matter and energy transfers are inside the accounting. State variables may include pressure, volume, temperature and composition. An equilibrium state is characterized by suitable macroscopic quantities; processes between states can exchange heat and work with surroundings.

== First law and sign convention ==
The first law expresses conservation of energy. Under the convention that Q is heat added to a closed system and W is work done by the system, the change in internal energy is

<pre>
ΔU = Q - W
</pre>

A different sign convention for work changes the written formula. Heat and work describe energy transfers during a process; internal energy is a state property. For open systems, transported energy accompanying mass flow must also be included.

== Second law and entropy ==
The second law constrains the directions and possibilities of macroscopic processes. Heat flows spontaneously from warmer to cooler bodies without external work; a refrigerator can move heat the other way by using work. For an isolated system, total thermodynamic entropy does not decrease. Entropy in thermodynamics has physical state definitions and units that should not be conflated automatically with [[Related to::Entropy|Shannon information entropy]].

== Examples and applications ==
Thermodynamic laws apply to heat engines, refrigeration, phase changes, chemical processes, atmospheric dynamics and biological energy conversion. An engine can conserve energy while losing some potential to produce useful work; [[Related to::Energy efficiency|energy efficiency]] requires a defined useful output and system boundary.

== Evidence and limitations ==
Model choice matters: ideal gases, closed systems and quasi-static processes are simplifying assumptions. State properties do not imply every real process stays in equilibrium, nor do introductory laws yield the detailed efficiency of a particular device without its operating conditions and physical model.

